('island-castles', 'osm-way-4010491', 'castle', 'Caisteal Chiosmuil', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kisimul_Castle', 'Kisimul Castle', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'the castle stands on this small island. Kisimul Castle.', 'Q79160', 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'mainland-shetland', 'tradition', 'Up Helly Aa', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Up_Helly_Aa', 'Up Helly Aa', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Up Helly Aa (Shaetlan pronunciation: [ˌɔp hɛli ˈaː] UP-hel-ee-AH; literally "Up Holy [Day] All") is a type of fire festival held annually from January to March in various communities in Shetland, Scotland, to mark the end of the Yule season. Each festival involves a torchlit procession by squads of costumed participants (known as guizers) that culminates in the burning of an imitation Viking galley.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'mainland-orkney', 'tradition', 'The Ba', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirkwall_Ba%27_Game', 'Kirkwall Ba'' Game', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Kirkwall Ba'' Game (known locally as The Ba'') is one of the main annual events held in the town of Kirkwall, in Orkney, Scotland. It is one of a number of Ba'' Games played in the streets of towns around Scotland; these are examples of medieval football games which are still played in towns in the United Kingdom and worldwide.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Hop-tu-Naa', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hop-tu-Naa', 'Hop-tu-Naa', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Hop-tu-Naa ( HOP choo NAY; Manx: Oie Houney; Irish: Oíche Shamhna [ˌiːçə ˈhəunˠə]) is a Celtic festival celebrated in the Isle of Man on 31 October. It is the celebration of the traditional Gaelic festival of Samhain, the start of winter.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Hunt the Wren', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunt_the_Wren', 'Hunt the Wren', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Hunt the Wren (Manx: Shelg yn Dreean) is a traditional custom carried out on the Isle of Man on 26 December, Saint Stephen''s Day. Traditionally, men and boys hunted a wren and placed it on top of a staff decorated with holly, ivy and ribbons, or displayed it in a decorated box on top of a pole.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Tynwald Day', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tynwald_Day', 'Tynwald Day', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Tynwald Day (Manx: Laa Tinvaal) is the National Day of the Isle of Man.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Yn Chruinnaght', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yn_Chruinnaght', 'Yn Chruinnaght', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Yn Chruinnaght (Manx for "the gathering") is a cultural festival in the Isle of Man which celebrates Manx music, Manx language and culture, and links with other Celtic cultures. The forerunner of Yn Chruinnaght was founded in 1924, and was known as Cruinnaght Vanninagh Ashoonagh ("Manx national gathering").', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-way-173383816', 'tradition', 'Papa Stour sword dance', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papa_Stour', 'Papa Stour', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Papa Stour sword dance may be of Norse origin and bears similarities to the long sword dance of the north east of England.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-8249591', 'tradition', 'Old Christmas', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foula', 'Foula', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'As a result, Foula is now one day ahead of the Julian calendar and 12 days behind the Gregorian, observing Christmas Day on 6 January Gregorian and New Year on 13 January Gregorian.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'fair-isle', 'tradition', 'Fair Isle knitting', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fair_Isle_%28knitting%29', 'Fair Isle (knitting)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Fair Isle (/fɛəraɪ̯l/) is a traditional knitting style used to create patterns with multiple colours. It is named after Fair Isle, one of the Shetland Islands.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-3601567', 'tradition', 'Festival of the Horse', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Ronaldsay', 'South Ronaldsay', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The village has a small blacksmith''s museum and is known for its annual Boys'' Ploughing Match. During this event young girls and boys dressed in dark jackets play the part of the horses, and young boys using miniature ploughs compete with one another at ploughing a 4-foot square rig in the nearby sands.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-way-4569485', 'tradition', 'North Ronaldsay sheep dyke', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Ronaldsay', 'North Ronaldsay', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'A 1.8 m (6 ft) dry stone dyke has been built to surround the island, the purpose of which is to keep the seaweed-eating local sheep off the arable land.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-way-4558807', 'tradition', 'King of Tory', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tory_Island', 'Tory Island', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'In keeping with a long-standing tradition, a "king" is chosen by consensus of the islanders.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Battle of Flowers', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey', 'Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Battle of Flowers is a carnival that has been held annually in August since 1902.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Black butter', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_butter', 'Black butter', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Known in Jèrriais as lé nièr beurre, a dark spicy spread prepared from apples, cider and spices (including liquorice), a traditional speciality of Jersey.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Jersey wonders', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey', 'Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Among other traditional dishes are cabbage loaf, Jersey wonders (les mèrvelles), fliottes, bean crock (les pais au fou), nettle (ortchie) soup, and vraic buns.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Clameur de Haro', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clameur_de_haro', 'Clameur de haro', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The clameur de haro (French pronunciation: [klamœʁ də aʁo]) is an ancient legal injunction of restraint employed by a person who believes they are being wronged by another at that moment. It survives as a fully enforceable law to this day in the legal systems of Jersey and Guernsey, and is used, albeit infrequently, for matters affecting land.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Clameur de Haro', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clameur_de_haro', 'Clameur de haro', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The clameur de haro (French pronunciation: [klamœʁ də aʁo]) is an ancient legal injunction of restraint employed by a person who believes they are being wronged by another at that moment. It survives as a fully enforceable law to this day in the legal systems of Jersey and Guernsey, and is used, albeit infrequently, for matters affecting land.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Viaer Marchi', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viaer_Marchi', 'Viaer Marchi', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Lé Viaer Marchi (Guernésiais: the old market) is an annual community festival held in Guernsey, generally on the first Monday of July. Lé Viaer Marchi is both a cultural show event, and the most important fundraising event for the National Trust of Guernsey.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Bean jar', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guernsey_bean_jar', 'Guernsey bean jar', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Bean jar (Guernésiais: moussaettes au four; French: pot de haricots de Guernesey) is a local dish of the Channel Island of Guernsey. The traditional Guernsey bean jar has been around for centuries, and is still popular today.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Guernsey sweater', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guernsey_%28clothing%29', 'Guernsey (clothing)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'A guernsey, or gansey, is a seaman''s knitted woollen sweater, similar to a jersey, which originated in the Channel Island of the same name, sometimes known as a knit-frock in Cornwall, especially Polperro.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'scilly-st-marys', 'tradition', 'Pilot gig racing', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Pilot_Gig_Championships', 'World Pilot Gig Championships', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The World Pilot Gig Championships are an annual gig racing event held on the Isles of Scilly, United Kingdom during the May Day bank holiday weekend.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-8815463', 'tradition', 'Stone skimming', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Easdale', 'Easdale', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The World Stone Skimming Championship has taken place annually in September on Easdale since 1997.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6045552', 'tradition', 'Aran knitting', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aran_jumper', 'Aran jumper', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Aran jumper (Irish: Geansaí Árann), also called a fisherman''s jumper or a gansey, is a style of jumper that takes its name from the Aran Islands off the west coast of Ireland. A traditional Aran Jumper usually is off-white in colour, with cable patterns on the body and sleeves.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'lewis-and-harris', 'tradition', 'Harris Tweed', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_tweed', 'Harris tweed', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Harris tweed (Scottish Gaelic: Clò mór or Clò hearach) is a tweed cloth that is handwoven by islanders at their homes in the Outer Hebrides of Scotland, finished in the Outer Hebrides, and made from pure virgin wool dyed and spun in the Outer Hebrides. This definition, quality standards and protection of the Harris tweed name are enshrined in the Harris Tweed Act 1993.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-380670', 'tradition', 'Harris Tweed', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_tweed', 'Harris tweed', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'When this trade mark, the Orb, was eventually granted, the board insisted that it should be granted to all the islands of the Outer Hebrides i.e. to Lewis, North and South Uist, Benbecula and Barra, as well as to Harris, the rationale for this decision being that the tweed was made in exactly the same way in all those islands.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-380646', 'tradition', 'Harris Tweed', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_tweed', 'Harris tweed', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'When this trade mark, the Orb, was eventually granted, the board insisted that it should be granted to all the islands of the Outer Hebrides i.e. to Lewis, North and South Uist, Benbecula and Barra, as well as to Harris, the rationale for this decision being that the tweed was made in exactly the same way in all those islands.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-way-20594001', 'tradition', 'The guga hunt', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sula_Sgeir', 'Sula Sgeir', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'How long before 1549 the Nessmen sailed to Sula Sgeir each year to collect the young gannets for food and feathers is not known, but it may be assumed that it was a tradition for centuries.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'lewis-and-harris', 'tradition', 'The guga hunt', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sula_Sgeir', 'Sula Sgeir', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'In 2018, BBC Alba aired Sulasgeir: An t-Sealg/The Hunt, a Gaelic-language documentary about the traditional annual guga hunt held on Sulasgeir by members of the community of Ness on Lewis.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'st-kilda', 'tradition', 'St Kilda parliament', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Kilda%2C_Scotland', 'St Kilda, Scotland', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Another important aspect of St Kildan life was the daily "parliament". This was a meeting held in the street every morning after prayers and attended by all the adult males during the course of which they would decide upon the day''s activities.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'st-kilda', 'tradition', 'St Kilda mailboat', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Kilda%2C_Scotland', 'St Kilda, Scotland', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Even in the late 19th century, the islanders could communicate with the rest of the world only by lighting a bonfire on the summit of Conachair which would, weather permitting, be visible to those on the isles of Harris and the Uists, or by using the "St Kilda mailboat".', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'islay', 'tradition', 'Fèis Ìle', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islay_single_malts', 'Islay single malts', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Islay is a centre of "whisky tourism", and hosts a "Festival of Malt and Music" known as Fèis Ìle each year on the last week of May, with events and tastings celebrating the cultural heritage of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-380646', 'tradition', 'Fèis Bharraigh', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A8is_Bharraigh', 'Fèis Bharraigh', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Fèis Bharraigh (Scottish Gaelic pronunciation: [ˌfeːʃˈvarˠaj]) is an annual Gaelic arts and culture event, held on Barra.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-3414078', 'tradition', 'Omey races', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omey_Island', 'Omey Island', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The beach is the site of the annual Omey Races, reestablished in 2001. This horse racing event is held in late summer (July/August).', null, 'high', 'accepted');
