('local-traditions', 'lewis-and-harris', 'tradition', 'Hebridean Celtic Festival', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebridean_Celtic_Festival', 'Hebridean Celtic Festival', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Hebridean Celtic Festival (Scottish Gaelic: Fèis Cheilteach Innse Gall) or HebCelt is an international Scottish music festival, which takes place annually in Stornoway on Lewis, in the Outer Hebrides of Scotland. The festival regularly attracts over 16,000 attendees and provides significant economic and cultural benefits for its host area. The picture shows the Hebridean Celtic Festival.', null, 'high', 'accepted');
insert into public.interest_memberships (category_slug, island_id, facet, title, source_url, source_title, licence, evidence, external_id, confidence, status) values
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6045455', 'tradition', 'Tiree Music Festival', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiree_Music_Festival', 'Tiree Music Festival', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Tiree Music Festival is a Scottish folk music festival held annually on the Island of Tiree in the Inner Hebrides. The festival was founded in 2010 by resident Stewart MacLennan and local musician Daniel Gillespie of the band Skerryvore. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-4619204', 'tradition', 'Isle of Jura Fell Race', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Jura_Fell_Race', 'Isle of Jura Fell Race', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Isle of Jura Fell Race is an annual fell race held in May, starting and finishing at Craighouse on the Scottish island of Jura. The course loops west and north over several hills including the Paps of Jura. The picture shows the Isle of Jura Fell Race.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'mainland-shetland', 'tradition', 'Delting Up Helly Aa', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delting_Up_Helly_Aa', 'Delting Up Helly Aa', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Delting Up Helly Aa (known as Brae Up Helly Aa until 2001) is the final festival in the Shetland Islands'' Up Helly Aa calendar, taking place on the third Friday of March in the parish of Delting. It has been held in the Mossbank Community Hall with the Manager of Sullom Voe Terminal since 1999. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'mainland-shetland', 'tradition', 'Ness yoal', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoal', 'Yoal', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The yoal (Shaetlan pronunciation: [joɐɫ] YOHL), often referred to as the ness yoal, is a clinker-built craft used traditionally in Shetland, Scotland. However, the Ness yoal is better regarded as a successful and sophisticated adaptation for the specific local requirements of the fishermen where these vessels were used which was Dunrossness, the southernmost parish of Mainland. The picture shows a ness yoal.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'fair-isle', 'tradition', 'Yoal', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoal', 'Yoal', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The yoal (Shaetlan pronunciation: [joɐɫ] YOHL), often referred to as the ness yoal, is a clinker-built craft used traditionally in Shetland, Scotland. Ian Best of Fair Isle, and Tommy Isbister of Trondra, are contemporary yoal builders. The picture shows a yoal.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-12658664', 'tradition', 'Yoal', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoal', 'Yoal', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The yoal (Shaetlan pronunciation: [joɐɫ] YOHL), often referred to as the ness yoal, is a clinker-built craft used traditionally in Shetland, Scotland. Ian Best of Fair Isle, and Tommy Isbister of Trondra, are contemporary yoal builders. The picture shows a yoal.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6045364', 'tradition', 'Currach', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Currach', 'Currach', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'A currach (Irish: curach [ˈkʊɾˠəx]) is a type of Irish boat with a wooden frame, over which animal skins or hides were once stretched, though now canvas is more usual. In Achill Island the currach is built with double gunwales. The picture shows a currach.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Cammag', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cammag', 'Cammag', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Cammag (Manx pronunciation: [ˈkʰamaɡ]) is a team sport originating on the Isle of Man. In modern times, an annual match of cammag is played in St John''s on 26 December in keeping with this tradition. The picture shows a cammag match.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-way-495525351', 'tradition', 'Curling-stone quarry', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ailsa_Craig', 'Ailsa Craig', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The island''s microgranite has long been quarried to make curling stones. Ailsa Craig produced two types of granite for curling, Blue Hone and Ailsa Craig Common Green. The picture shows the curling-stone quarries on Ailsa Craig.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6046655', 'tradition', 'King of Bardsey', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardsey_Island', 'Bardsey Island', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Bardsey Island (Welsh: Ynys Enlli), known as the legendary "Island of 20,000 Saints", is located 1.9 miles (3.1 km) off the Llŷn Peninsula in the Welsh county of Gwynedd. It was tradition for the island to elect the "King of Bardsey" (Welsh: Brenin Enlli), and from 1826 onwards, he would be crowned by Baron Newborough or his representative. The picture shows the King of Bardsey.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6045499', 'tradition', 'Fetlar sheepdog trials', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fetlar', 'Fetlar', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Fetlar is one of the North Isles of Shetland, Scotland, with a usually resident population of 61 at the time of the 2011 census and 66 in 2022. The Fetlar sheepdog trials take place annually, normally in July. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'osm-relation-6045499', 'tradition', 'Fetlar Foy', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fetlar', 'Fetlar', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Fetlar Foy, once very popular with Shetlanders and tourists alike, took place at midsummer on the Links at Tresta where folk were entertained with music, food and drink. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Guernsey Gâche', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guernsey_G%C3%A2che', 'Guernsey Gâche', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Guernsey Gâche () is a dish of the Channel Island of Guernsey. It is a special bread made with raisins, sultanas, butter and mixed peel. The picture shows Guernsey gâche.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Jersey Eisteddfod', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey_Eisteddfod', 'Jersey Eisteddfod', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Jersey Eisteddfod is a cultural festival and competition in Jersey. It was founded in 1908 by former Dean of Jersey Samuel Falle, who saw its competitive classes as a means by which the speech, presentation, and musical standards of his fellow islanders might be improved. The picture shows the Jersey Eisteddfod.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Liberation Day', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_Day_(Channel_Islands)', 'Liberation Day (Channel Islands)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Liberation Day (Jèrriais: Jour d''la Libéthâtion; Guernésiais: Lé Jour dé la Libératiaon) is the national day of both Guernsey and Jersey, the two largest of the Channel Islands, which takes place on 9 May each year. 9 May is a public holiday in both islands and each has different celebrations and commemorative events; the centrepiece of Jersey''s is the Liberation Day re-enactment in the Liberation Square, while Guernsey''s is an islandwide cavalcade of classic vehicles. The picture shows Liberation Day in Jersey.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Liberation Day', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberation_Day_(Channel_Islands)', 'Liberation Day (Channel Islands)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Liberation Day (Jèrriais: Jour d''la Libéthâtion; Guernésiais: Lé Jour dé la Libératiaon) is the national day of both Guernsey and Jersey, the two largest of the Channel Islands, which takes place on 9 May each year. 9 May is a public holiday in both islands and each has different celebrations and commemorative events; the centrepiece of Jersey''s is the Liberation Day re-enactment in the Liberation Square, while Guernsey''s is an islandwide cavalcade of classic vehicles. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'guernsey', 'tradition', 'Vale Earth Fair', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vale_Earth_Fair', 'Vale Earth Fair', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Vale Earth Fair is an annual festival held on the Channel Island of Guernsey that promotes music and the arts, and is the longest running such festival in the Channel Islands. Launched in 1976, the festival takes place each year at Vale Castle, and is organised by a group of volunteers. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Isle of Man TT', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_the_Isle_of_Man', 'Culture of the Isle of Man', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The main international event associated with the island is the Isle of Man Tourist Trophy race, colloquially known as "The TT", which began in 1907. Taking place over a two-week period, it has become a festival for motorcycling culture, makes a huge contribution to the island''s economy and has become part of Manx identity. The picture shows the Isle of Man TT.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Visite du Branchage', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Jersey', 'Culture of Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Annual visites du branchage are carried out twice in summer by parish officials to inspect roadside verges and hedges and ensure property owners have trimmed back overhanging greenery. This custom is to prevent Jersey''s narrow lanes becoming hazardous or impassable through overgrown vegetation. The picture shows the visite du branchage.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Cabbage loaf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Jersey', 'Culture of Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Cabbage loaf is the traditional Jersey bread baked between two cabbage leaves. The picture shows a Jersey cabbage loaf.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Tall Jacks', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Jersey', 'Culture of Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Historically, Jersey produced sturdy walking sticks fashioned from the stalks of cabbages, known as "Tall Jacks", which had been induced to grow tall stalks by removing leaves around the heart. The picture shows Jersey cabbage walking sticks.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Vraicking', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Jersey', 'Culture of Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Vraic is the Jersey word for seaweed, and the collection of seaweed for fertiliser, vraicking, an important activity in the past, still continues on a small scale. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'jersey', 'tradition', 'Good Friday picnic', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Jersey', 'Culture of Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'On Good Fridays of the past, it was tradition for Jerseymen to travel for a picnic. Limpets would be collected from the rocks and cooked on the spot with bread cake eaten hot with butter. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('local-traditions', 'isle-of-man', 'tradition', 'Spuds and herrin', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_the_Isle_of_Man', 'Culture of the Isle of Man', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Traditionally the national dish of the island is "spuds and herrin", boiled potatoes and herring. This plain dish is chosen because of its role supporting the subsistence farmers of the island, who crofted the land and fished the sea for centuries. Photograph of the island.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'mainland-orkney', 'silent', 'Stromness distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'List of whisky distilleries in Scotland', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia''s list of former malt distilleries gives the row Stromness, location Orkney, region Highland, year closed 1928, demolished 1940. The Orkney article says Stromness is on the Mainland, and that Kirkwall and Stromness are on that island. Wikidata Q2356880 is at 58.9578,-3.3021, the town of Stromness.', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'high', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'mainland-orkney', 'distillery', 'Orkney distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'List of whisky distilleries in Scotland', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The June 2026 operating malt table names Orkney at Kirkwall, Island region. The Orkney article places Kirkwall on the Mainland. The Island single malt page also lists Orkney distillery on Orkney, under distilleries in development, so this row is medium rather than high.', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'medium', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'osm-relation-380684', 'distillery', 'North Uist distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'List of whisky distilleries in Scotland', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The operating malt table lists North Uist separately from Benbecula distillery, with the location cell Benbecula. Benbecula is the atlas island of that name. There is no separate prose article.', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_Scotland', 'medium', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'isle-of-wight', 'distillery', 'Isle of Wight Distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'List of whisky distilleries in England', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The England whisky-distillery article says the Isle of Wight Distillery started distilling whisky in December 2015 and released a whisky in December 2018. Its table gives the town as Ryde, the port town on the Isle of Wight.', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'high', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'isle-of-wight', 'planned', 'Wight Distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'List of whisky distilleries in England', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The England list''s town cell for Wight Distillery is Isle of Wight, distinct from the 2015 Isle of Wight Distillery row at Ryde. The first-release year is 2027, so this row is filed as not yet releasing whisky.', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'medium', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'scilly-st-marys', 'distillery', 'Scilly Isles Distillery', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'List of whisky distilleries in England', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The England whisky list names Scilly Isles Distillery at St Mary''s, first release 2024. The name Scilly Isles distinguishes this St Mary''s from the other St Mary''s islands in the atlas. The atlas id is scilly-st-marys, St Mary''s (Isles of Scilly).', 'List_of_whisky_distilleries_in_England', 'medium', 'accepted'),
('whisky-distilleries', 'isle-of-skye', 'story', 'Illicit stills on Skye', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talisker_distillery', 'Talisker distillery', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Talisker distillery article places seven registered distilleries and many illicit operations on Skye. It does not name the other five registered stills, so they are not separate rows.', 'Talisker_distillery', 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-way-4569485', 'wildlife', 'blonde hedgehog', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_hedgehog', 'European hedgehog', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The source names blonde hedgehogs and says they are extremely rare except on North Ronaldsay and Alderney.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-relation-380685', 'wildlife', 'Dactylorhiza francis-drucei var. ebudensis', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dactylorhiza_francis-drucei', 'Dactylorhiza francis-drucei', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The article gives var. ebudensis the range North Uist, and gives the other accepted taxa wider ranges.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'isle-of-skye', 'wildlife', 'Alpine rock-cress', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabis_alpina', 'Arabis alpina', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The article says that in the British Isles Arabis alpina is only known from the Cuillin Ridge of the Isle of Skye.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-relation-7230308', 'wildlife', 'Edwardsia delapiae', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwardsia_delapiae', 'Edwardsia delapiae', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The article says the anemone is currently only known from Valentia harbour, and the word Valentia is linked to the Valentia Island article.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-relation-2856487', 'wildlife', 'Atlantic puffin', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skomer', 'Skomer', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Skomer article says the Atlantic puffin colony is the largest in southern Britain.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-way-1043462669', 'wildlife', 'Pied avocet', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Havergate_Island', 'Havergate Island', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Havergate Island article says the UK''s largest breeding population of pied avocets can be found there. The atlas record is the only Havergate island, and its area matches the island described.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'isle-of-wight', 'wildlife', 'Red squirrel', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_squirrel', 'Red squirrel', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The red squirrel article calls the Isle of Wight the largest haven in England.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'osm-way-236730548', 'wildlife', 'Roccella fuciformis', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annet,_Isles_of_Scilly', 'Annet, Isles of Scilly', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Annet article calls the island the British stronghold for the lichen Roccella fuciformis.', null, 'high', 'accepted'),
('unusual-wildlife', 'jersey', 'wildlife', 'wall lizard', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jersey', 'Jersey', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'The Jersey article says Jersey is the only native habitat in the British Isles for the wall lizard.', null, 'high', 'accepted');
